WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:05.250
The building blocks of urbanism are
things that started many, many tens of

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:11.219
thousands of years ago. One of them in
particular is language: the possibility

00:00:11.219 --> 00:00:16.020
of speaking, not only to communicate
information, but also to think about

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:22.230
things that are not there, like the past and the future—both of

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:28.260
which are entirely imaginary constructs,
but for which we have the language to be

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:34.469
able to envision something that is not
there. Even more important is the

00:00:34.469 --> 00:00:39.480
opportunity to put in a conditional
tense, not just a past, present, and a

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:47.340
future, but a 'what if?' 'If we build it,
they will come.' That kind of planning is

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:52.739
something that was absolutely essential, not only to our long human survival, but to

00:00:52.739 --> 00:00:57.300
the eventual possibility of living in
larger and larger configurations of

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:04.460
strangers. Another thing that humans are
very good about is dealing with objects.

00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:13.110
We're the only species that has stuff,
and certainly has so much stuff. And we

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:18.020
have been trying to invent new things
since the very beginning. The first

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:23.700
artifacts of any detailed aesthetic
consequence were from about a million

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:30.420
and a half years ago. And those artifacts
were made in quantities that suggested

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:37.110
that mere utility was not enough as part
of the human composition of making the

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:42.119
world around them, of making tools, for
example. The first artifacts were things

00:01:42.119 --> 00:01:46.950
called hand axes. And they look a little
bit like the two palms of your hand put

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:51.540
together. And we'd have a hard time
making one even today with our

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:56.100
sophisticated technical skills, because
it requires a certain amount of

00:01:56.100 --> 00:02:01.649
imagination to turn a stone over and
over again and manufacture them without

00:02:01.649 --> 00:02:08.729
hurting yourself. And the amount of work
that goes into these objects is far

00:02:08.729 --> 00:02:12.830
beyond what would be required to make a
useful sharp-edged tool.

00:02:12.830 --> 00:02:16.250
In fact, there's been some interesting
thought about the way that people

00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:21.380
manufactured those hand axes, not only to
be something useful, but to be a kind of

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:28.850
social calling card. 'Hey baby. Look at my
hand axe.' Another thing that's really

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:32.900
important for humans, and that has been
part of our makeup for a long time, has

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:39.080
been migration. So more than a million
years ago, our ancestors walked out of

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:44.390
Africa and into every corner of the old
world. That process of walking is still

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:52.640
there. Migration is not a new phenomenon;
it's a human phenomenon. And the last

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:56.360
thing that humans do that became a
precursor for the kinds of things we

00:02:56.360 --> 00:03:02.480
needed to become urban is the
development of architecture, of not just

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:08.030
accepting the world as it is, of being
able to find a shelter under a tree or

00:03:08.030 --> 00:03:15.320
in a cave, but to actually make
structures that suit people, not only for

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:21.140
shelter but also as a kind of social
calling card, as a way of putting

00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:26.120
together a household and making it
visible to the rest of the people around.

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:31.280
So those are the things that are the  building blocks of what it took

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:36.950
to be able to be urban. But many of those
things were done for tens of thousands

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:43.690
of years before people actually became
an urban species. And the first move

00:03:43.690 --> 00:03:50.450
towards getting people into larger and
larger groups was probably something

00:03:50.450 --> 00:03:57.470
that was a ritual impetus. The idea of
coming together and gathering with a

00:03:57.470 --> 00:04:02.950
bunch of strangers with something that
is evident in the earliest physical

00:04:02.950 --> 00:04:07.100
ritual structures that we have in the
world today. So you see here Stonehenge,

00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:11.690
which of course is very familiar to us,
and very picturesque and evocative. And

00:04:11.690 --> 00:04:16.040
then you also have Gobekli Tepe, which is
probably the world's earliest ritual

00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:20.000
structure in Turkey. Made by
hunter-gatherers who were coming

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:26.160
together and constructing
upright stone circles over and over

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:30.690
again to be able to make a place for
people to come together and have a

00:04:30.690 --> 00:04:35.870
ritual. And we know archaeologically that
these are places where people came

00:04:35.870 --> 00:04:42.540
repeatedly over and over again. They came,
they had celebrations, they had fires,

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:48.680
they did cooking, they interred their
dead. And all of that was very exciting,

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:55.830
including probably also people coming
along with trinkets to sell, or also, you

00:04:55.830 --> 00:05:00.510
know, people coming along for a little
romance. Down in the village

00:05:00.510 --> 00:05:05.520
where there were only 50 or 60 people at
any given time, marriage prospects were

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:09.930
probably rather limited. So the idea of
going to a ritual event with

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:13.830
something like a festival atmosphere, so
that you could have a little shopping, a

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:17.970
little ritual, a little romance, all at
the same time. But there was a

00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:21.840
catch about these ritual centers. And
that is, that after you were done with

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:27.660
the ritual, you were supposed to go home.
So when we think about places like

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:32.550
Stonehenge, we think about places like
Gobekli Tepe, there are still no cities

00:05:32.550 --> 00:05:39.960
anywhere near them. So it took something
more in order to be able to get people

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:47.040
to live in a place that was kind of like
a permanent festival atmosphere. And once

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:52.770
that started, once you had a kind of
economy of people living together, of all

00:05:52.770 --> 00:05:58.500
kinds of new opportunities for romance,
for education, for medical care—that's

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:00.520
when the first cities got going.

00:06:00.520 --> 00:06:04.180
 

